A||||9||RobertVol||Brake master cylinder question||||1||
Z||000000||RobertVol||09-23-2000||05:23 PM||||The pedal did that straight to the floor thing and I thought her we go ! <P>Master cylinder time. Any suggestions on best master cylinder for the 88 GT? <P>The guy at the shop said $120.00  for brand new ac delco. I said uhhhhh NO! I dont think so. I know you can get the one at the fierostore. I believe its ac/delco. I believe its like 60 - 70 dollars.  Any problems with that one? <P>------------------<BR>Robert<BR>88GT Top 3.8 SC t top <BR>88GT T top 55k for sale<BR>  <A HREF="http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros" TARGET=_blank>http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros</A>  <BR>          <IMG SRC="http://www.fortunecity.com/marina/strand/1086/3eab6a20.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||09-24-2000||01:01 AM||||Get a MC for a full sized Chevy Blazer (2 styles--get the one with the separate reservoir--you'll see what I mean when you look at it).  The bore is something like 5mm larger on that one.  Works great with my Beretta brakes.  Should improve your stock brakes as well.  It cost less than $25US, too.  If you have a sunroof, you'll need to swap reservoirs with the stock Fiero one in order to have enough clearance for your sunroof under the hood.  (I don't take my sunroof out, so I didn't bother swapping them.)||||reg||1
Z||000002||RobertVol||09-24-2000||01:17 AM||||Thanks,<P>Any specific yr ? of Full size blazer? ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-24-2000||02:40 PM||||Actually MM increasing the bore of the master likely reduces stock brake power. It's a basic hydraulic rule. The closer the 2 pistons are to the same size the more force needed on the master to do the same work on the slave.<P>It works with the upgraded brakes because the calipers also have larger bores.<P>You should be able to get Fiero master cylinders any place. The MC isn't anyhting special. Allot of times rebuilt units don't come with the plastic reserve tanks on. You are expected to use the existing one.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||09-25-2000||06:17 PM||||Ogre--I see what you're saying, but wouldn't a larger bore push more fluid?  All I know is that it certainly helped the Grape Ape.  (I drove it around the block after the brakes and before the new MC.  Seems like I could tell a difference, but maybe it's like you said.  Caliper pistons are larger and "match" the larger bore of the MC.)||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||09-25-2000||07:30 PM||||It would push more fluid, but it does it the wrong way. You can push more fluid by increasing stroke length or by increasing piston. Increasing stroke moves more fuild but keeps pedal effort the same. Increasing the piston increses pedal effort. <P>To make it really easy to explain.... ((I think this explanation is right.... long day.))<P>lets say theres a 10:1 difference between the slave and master. Every 1# we push on the master delivers 10# at the slave piston face.<P>If we change the diferance by increasing master diameter and make it 10:2(5:1) we now have to push 2# on the master to get 10# at the slave.<P>With the upgrade brakes you increase both master and slave. The ratio ends up a little wider than it started. (Since we can't change the stroke lenght much we have to increase MC piston diameter.)<P>Again for example... Lets say the stock brakes were 10:1 to start. With the new brakes and the stock MC it ended up 15:1. With the blazer MC it's now 12:1. (To figure the true ratio you need to know all the piston diameters.)<P>The only reason the old MC doesn't work is the amount of travel you need to move enough fluid. If you could get the fluid volume you'd actually need less effort on the stock MC than the blazer one. (The higher the ratio the less effort at the master.)||||reg||1
Z||000006||RobertVol||09-25-2000||08:07 PM||||Ohhhhh! OK <P>he he well thanks guys as always!<P>I went with the Re man MC through the Fierostore for 47 - 48 bucks. If thats all I am out oh well. I spend more than that on a long friday night  "hickup"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ||||reg||6
Z||000007||StuGood||09-25-2000||11:41 PM||||Ogre's right.  Larger MC piston means you move more fluid, but you get less leverage.||||reg||14
